Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Gothic period live-action.

FADE IN:

INT. BARE ROOM - NIGHT

A single candle lights the NARRATOR, a gaunt young adult with disordered dark hair, a bloodless face, and a buttoned black waistcoat over a white shirt. The bare walls seem to press inward.

The NARRATOR leans toward us, listening to something beyond hearing.

NARRATOR
True! — nervous — very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

The candle flame trembles. The NARRATOR's sharp eyes follow its faint hiss.

NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses — not destroyed — not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

A distant clock begins to tick.

NARRATOR
How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily — how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

A dark, old-fashioned chamber with heavy shutters, a broad bed, and bare wooden floorboards.

The OLD MAN, frail and white-haired in a gray nightshirt and robe, sits beside the NARRATOR. His pale blue right eye is clouded by a milky film.

The NARRATOR serves him tea with elaborate kindness. The OLD MAN looks up. That veiled eye settles upon the NARRATOR.

The NARRATOR's hand freezes around the cup.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

The OLD MAN smiles with innocent affection.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire.

The clouded eye remains fixed upon the NARRATOR.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! He had the eye of a vulture — a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

The NARRATOR forces a warm smile and refills the OLD MAN's cup.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very gradually, I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. BARE ROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR sits perfectly composed, hands folded.

NARRATOR
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen how wisely I proceeded — with what caution, with what foresight, with what dissimulation I went to work.

A slight smile appears.

NARRATOR
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. The shutters are fastened. Darkness fills the chamber.

The latch turns by a fraction. The door opens silently.

The NARRATOR eases a closed tin lantern through the gap, then slowly inserts their head. The OLD MAN sleeps beneath the bedclothes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it — oh, so gently.

The NARRATOR takes an agonizingly slow step into the chamber.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed.

The lantern shutter opens a hair. A thread of light crosses the OLD MAN's face, but his eye is closed.

The NARRATOR withdraws.

On another night, the same latch turns. The same head advances. The same thin ray finds the eye closed.

Night after night, the ritual repeats. The ticking clock marks seven midnights. Each time the clouded eye remains shut.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This I did for seven long nights — every night just at midnight — but I found the eye always closed. It was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light leaks around the shutters.

The NARRATOR strides in cheerfully with a breakfast tray. The OLD MAN sits awake in bed.

NARRATOR
Good morning. How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN takes the offered cup. His clouded eye regards the NARRATOR without suspicion.

The NARRATOR smiles broadly.

INT. BARE ROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR pantomimes turning a tiny latch between finger and thumb.

NARRATOR
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

The smile becomes triumphant.

NARRATOR
Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers — of my sagacity.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth midnight.

The NARRATOR opens the chamber door little by little. A soft chuckle escapes.

The OLD MAN moves suddenly beneath the covers.

The NARRATOR stops, then continues pressing the door inward.

A thumb slips on the lantern's tin fastening.

CLICK.

The OLD MAN springs upright.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The NARRATOR stands motionless in the black doorway, scarcely breathing.

The OLD MAN remains upright, staring into darkness.

The clock ticks. A deathwatch beetle answers from inside the wall.

Time crawls. The candle on the bedside table has long since burned out. Neither figure moves.

A slight, stifled groan rises from the OLD MAN.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

The OLD MAN grips the bedclothes, trying to master his fear.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been trying to fancy his fears causeless. It is nothing but the wind in the chimney. It is only a mouse crossing the floor. It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp.

The NARRATOR's white face hovers within the doorway.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he had found all in vain. Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him and enveloped the victim.

The lantern shutter begins to open — stealthily, stealthily.

A single dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, reaches across the chamber.

It falls precisely upon the OLD MAN's pale blue eye.

The eye is wide, wide open.

The NARRATOR stares at the hideous veil over it. Fury replaces caution.

A low, dull, quick THUMP begins.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense. There came to my ears a sound such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

THUMP. THUMP. THUMP.

The OLD MAN cannot see the intruder beyond the narrow ray. His mouth opens, but terror holds back his voice.

The NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless. One hand trembles. The other tightens upon the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

The heartbeat quickens.

The NARRATOR scarcely breathes. Sweat rolls down the pallid face.

The OLD MAN's fingers claw at the sheets.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For some minutes longer I refrained and stood still. But the beating grew louder — louder!

The heartbeat pounds through the dreadful silence of the house.

The NARRATOR glances toward the adjoining wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A new anxiety seized me — the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The heartbeat becomes thunderous.

NARRATOR
The old man's hour had come!

The NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the chamber.

The OLD MAN shrieks once.

The NARRATOR drags him onto the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

The heartbeat continues beneath the bed — muffled, rapid, relentless.

The NARRATOR kneels beside it, smiling.

THUMP. THUMP.

Minutes pass. The NARRATOR's smile fades into intense concentration.

The heartbeat slows.

One final THUMP.

Silence.

The NARRATOR pulls the bed aside and examines the motionless OLD MAN. A hand presses against his chest and remains there.

NARRATOR
Stone, stone dead.

The clouded eye stares without life.

NARRATOR
His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. BARE ROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR's expression is serene again.

NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

The ticking resumes, faster now.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - BEFORE DAWN

The NARRATOR works hastily but silently.

A sheet is drawn over the corpse. A blade flashes in candlelight. The grisly labor is hidden behind the hanging edge of the bed.

Wrapped sections of the body lie beside a wooden tub. No blood reaches the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The NARRATOR pries up three floorboards and lowers the wrapped remains between the joists.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye — not even his — could have detected anything wrong.

The last plank settles into place. The NARRATOR rubs dust into its seams and surveys the spotless chamber.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out — no stain of any kind — no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all — ha! ha!

A distant bell strikes four.

Three firm knocks sound from below.

INT. FRONT HALL - BEFORE DAWN

The NARRATOR opens the street door with the lantern raised.

OFFICER REYNOLDS, stern and middle-aged in a dark police coat, stands with OFFICER HALE, a broad, bearded constable, and OFFICER BELL, a lean constable with close-cropped gray hair.

The officers remove their hats and present their badges. The NARRATOR welcomes them with an easy smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play had been aroused, and they had been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search — search well.

The NARRATOR leads the officers upstairs without hesitation.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - BEFORE DAWN

The officers inspect the shutters, the bed, and the OLD MAN's undisturbed chest of valuables.

The NARRATOR brings in four chairs and places one directly over the replaced floorboards.

OFFICER REYNOLDS closes the chest. The three officers settle into their chairs, satisfied.

The NARRATOR sits above the hidden remains.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Everything appears secure.

They chat quietly of familiar things. The NARRATOR answers cheerily.

A faint ringing creeps into the room.

The NARRATOR's smile tightens.

A low, dull, quick THUMP begins beneath the conversation.

The officers remain at ease.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears. But it continued and gained definiteness, until I found that the noise was not within my ears.

THUMP. THUMP. THUMP.

The NARRATOR speaks more rapidly, gesturing through a strained explanation. The officers exchange polite smiles.

The heartbeat grows louder.

The NARRATOR rises and paces across the floorboards.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath — and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly, more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

The officers continue their pleasant conversation.

The NARRATOR argues about trifles in a high voice, hands slicing the air.

THUMP. THUMP. THUMP.

The NARRATOR drags the chair across the boards with a shriek of wood against wood.

The heartbeat rises above it.

The officers fall silent, watching with courteous concern.

NARRATOR
Why will you not be gone?

OFFICER REYNOLDS
You appear unwell.

The NARRATOR paces with heavy strides, pale and gasping.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They heard! — they suspected! — they knew! They were making a mockery of my horror!

OFFICER HALE offers a mild, reassuring smile.

The NARRATOR recoils from it.

The heartbeat pounds louder — louder — louder.

The NARRATOR clutches both sides of the head, then stares down at the exact place beneath the chair.

THUMP. THUMP. THUMP.

The NARRATOR kicks the chair away.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!

The officers leap to their feet.

The NARRATOR drops to both knees and claws at the seams of the floorboards.

NARRATOR
Tear up the planks! Here, here! It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers seize the NARRATOR.

The heartbeat stops.

In the sudden silence, the NARRATOR presses an ear against the floor and listens.

INT. BARE ROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR sits where the confession began. The waistcoat is disordered now, the white shirt damp with sweat.

The candle gutters toward extinction.

NARRATOR
Do you mark me well?

The NARRATOR listens.

From somewhere beneath the bare floor comes one low, muffled THUMP.

The candle goes out.

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
